"A strong typhoon has a strange thing at its center: an eye. Inside the eye there is almost no wind and no rain, and you may even see the sun or the stars. But right at the edge of the eye is a ring of thick cloud where the wind and rain are the strongest of all. From there outward, they slowly grow weaker.": "07662716d760",
"A typhoon begins over a warm tropical sea. The sun heats the water, and a lot of it turns into water vapor. The air above the sea becomes warm and moist, so it is light, and it rises. Cooler air from all around flows in to take its place, and then it rises too. The air pressure in the middle becomes lower and lower.": "d1cd4ec7f659",
"A typhoon cannot live without warm sea. When it moves over land, there is far less water vapor to feed it, and mountains and rough ground break up its shape. It grows weaker, and in the end it fades away. Most typhoons last from four or five days to a little more than ten. That is long enough to cross an ocean, so the safest thing we can do is follow the warnings and get ready early.": "8acd89670f51",
"A typhoon is coming this weekend.": "4dfe63efb7dd",
"A typhoon warning was sent out this morning.": "7673de2a0f20",
"Every summer and fall, people in Taiwan watch the weather report closely. Somewhere far out over the Pacific, a patch of cloud has begun to turn. A few days later it may arrive as a typhoon, with wind strong enough to pull up trees and rain enough to fill a river in hours.": "7c1accd22efe",
"Every summer and fall, people in Taiwan watch the weather report closely. Somewhere far out over the Pacific, a patch of cloud has begun to turn. A few days later it may arrive as a typhoon, with wind strong enough to pull up trees and rain enough to fill a river in hours. A typhoon begins over a warm tropical sea. The sun heats the water, and a lot of it turns into water vapor. The air above the sea becomes warm and moist, so it is light, and it rises. Cooler air from all around flows in to take its place, and then it rises too. The air pressure in the middle becomes lower and lower. The air does not flow straight to the middle. Because the Earth spins, the air curves, and it begins to circle the center. North of the equator, it turns counterclockwise. Now the storm has a shape: long bands of cloud winding in toward the middle. Then the storm starts to feed itself. As the moist air rises, its water vapor cools and turns into cloud and rain. This change gives out heat. The heat makes the air rise even faster, which pulls in more moist air from the sea. When the wind near the center reaches 17.2 meters a second, which is about 62 kilometers an hour, we call the storm a typhoon. A strong typhoon has a strange thing at its center: an eye. Inside the eye there is almost no wind and no rain, and you may even see the sun or the stars. But right at the edge of the eye is a ring of thick cloud where the wind and rain are the strongest of all. From there outward, they slowly grow weaker. A typhoon cannot live without warm sea. When it moves over land, there is far less water vapor to feed it, and mountains and rough ground break up its shape. It grows weaker, and in the end it fades away. Most typhoons last from four or five days to a little more than ten. That is long enough to cross an ocean, so the safest thing we can do is follow the warnings and get ready early.": "05d971d6f8e2",
